# The annotations of the game image: the single source of truth for src/game/*.asm (kit/mkgame.py).
# The format is in kit/mkgame.py, class Annotations. One line an entry:
#
#   @module NAME ; title            the entries from here on go to NAME.asm, the title heads the file
#   @about a paragraph              the module file's header text, one paragraph a line
#   $ADDR c Name ; title            a routine
#   > a description line
#   $ADDR b Name ; title            data (w words, t text, s space; "b Name 8" rows of 8)
#   @res graphics/NAME.bin          the entry is a resource: src/res/..., INCBIN
#   $ADDR Name ; comment            a label and comment inside an entry: on an instruction, or on the data line
#                                   that starts at ADDR (a data entry's rows are cut there)
#   @isub $ADDR LD HL,-6            the instruction written so
#   @equ $ADDR Name [N] ; comment    a variable past the image: an EQU, its operands by name
#   @inline $ADDR                   a routine that reads its data after the CALL

@module viewtail ; the tail of the view buffer the game block loads over
@about The game block unpacks to $6731-$D7D7, so its first 79 bytes land in the last lines of ViewBuffer ($5B00-$677F, 20 bytes x 160 lines, the play view's back buffer). ClearViewBuffer clears the whole buffer before the first view is drawn, so these bytes are never read: a leftover of the block's start, kept byte for byte.
$6731 b ViewBufferTail 16 ; The last 79 bytes of ViewBuffer as the block loads them (lines 156-159, from byte 1 of line 156)
> Never read: DrawView clears ViewBuffer ($5B00-$677F) before it draws, and nothing else reads this part before that. A leftover of how the block was cut, kept as it is.

@module music ; the AY music in the interrupts, the game's bank paging, the menu picture
@about The game pages four other 16 K banks at $C000 while it runs: bank 1 (the tunes and their player), bank 3 (Dead End and the menu picture's code), bank 4 (the disk module) and bank 6 (the Final Cut block). Its own bank 0, which holds the code from $C000, the font, the radar, the robots and the map, is paged back every time (port $7FFD = $10: bank 0, screen 5, the 48 BASIC ROM).
@about Two interrupt handlers play the tune, one frame a call, through the player's frame entry PlayerFrame ($C005 in bank 1): MusicInterrupt outside play (the main menu, the construction menu) and PlayMusic in play, which PlayInterrupt reaches by a JP in place of its CALL ShowRadar. Each pages bank 1 for the player and then pages back the bank in GameBank, so a frame of music can fall while bank 3 is paged.
@about SetGameBank is the only writer of GameBank: MenuPicture pages bank 3 through it while bank 3's code draws the menu picture, on a stack in the code area (GlueStack, below GameEntry) because the game's own stack lies under the paged bank. The disk calls and the endings page their banks directly, after a HALT, with no music handler left to run in between.
$6780 c MusicInterrupt ; The interrupt handler outside play: one frame of the tune
> Installed in ImHandler by Restart ($A614) for the main menu and by ConstructionMenu ($C85E) while the construction menu is open (and by the unused SaveGame and GameStop). Keeps every register, the alternate set and IX, IY too, since the player in bank 1 uses them all: pages bank 1 ($11), calls PlayerFrame, pages back the bank in GameBank. No frame count, no radar: the world stands while it is in place.
$6780 ; (the CPU has already turned interrupts off on acceptance)
$678F ; bank 1: the tunes and their player
$6796 ; one frame of the tune
$679C ; the bank the game had paged
$67AF ; interrupts on again
$67B1 c PlayMusic ; The music in play: PlayInterrupt's radar call, then one frame of the tune
> PlayInterrupt jumps here at $D582 in place of its CALL ShowRadar: ShowRadar runs as before, then bank 1's player plays a frame (the main registers and IX, IY kept), the bank in GameBank goes back, and the JP returns to PlayInterrupt after its call ($D585: the sound effect step, the exit). Bug: AF' is not kept, and the player swaps it 10-12 times a frame; FindTargetBuilding keeps the order in A' over a table walk longer than a frame, so the walk's teammate check reads the player's value after the interrupt (trace d1 14257-14258: the order 6 read as 56 for records 21-23).
$67B1 ; this frame's radar picture, as PlayInterrupt set it up
$67BC ; bank 1
$67C3 ; one frame of the tune
$67C9 ; the game's bank again
$67D6 ; on in PlayInterrupt: the sound effect, the exit
$67D9 b GameBank ; The $7FFD value the game last set: $10 (bank 0, screen 5, the 48 BASIC ROM)
> Written only by SetGameBank, read by both music handlers to page the game's bank back after the player. $13 while MenuPicture runs bank 3's code.
$67DA c SetGameBank ; Page the bank in A ($7FFD) and keep it in GameBank
> BC kept. Called by MenuPicture: bank 3 ($13) for the picture, bank 0 ($10) after it.
$67E5 c MenuPicture ; The main menu's picture: bank 3's code draws it
> Called by MainMenuTitle ($C103). Bank 3 lies over $C000-$FFFF, the game's stack with it, so the picture code runs on GlueStack (SP = $A5ED); the old SP is kept in the operand of the LD SP at $67F9. Bank 3's MenuPictureCode ($E73B) unpacks the picture onto the screen; then bank 0 again.
$67E5 ; keep SP in the LD SP below
$67E9 ; GlueStack: the bank-3 call needs a stack below $C000
$67EC ; bank 3: Dead End and the picture
$67F1 ; the picture onto the screen
$67F4 ; bank 0, the game's own, again
$67F9 MenuPictureSP ; the old SP (self-modified operand)
$67FD s MusicSpare ; 3 bytes, never used

@module im2 ; the IM 2 table and jump, and the disk save's bridge between them
@about The game runs in IM 2 with I = $68. Restart fills ImTable ($6800-$6900, 257 bytes) with $69, so whatever the data bus holds at the interrupt the vector read is $6969, where Restart has put a JP (ImJump) whose operand ImHandler ($696A) is the only thing that changes: MusicInterrupt outside play, PlayInterrupt in play. The table and the jump lie below $C000, where no paged bank can cover them, and are zero in the game block.
@about Between them sits GameSave, the save half of the disk bridge (the load half, GameLoad, is in the disk module of this source, at $A56A): it pages bank 4 and jumps into the disk module, which comes back to SaveReturn.
$6800 s ImTable ; The IM 2 vector table: $6800-$6900, 257 bytes of $69 written by Restart; zero in the block
> Restart writes the 257 bytes ($6800 + 256 and the one after) every time it runs. The four bytes $6901-$6904 after the table are never written or read.
$6905 c GameSave ; Save the game through the disk module in bank 4, then back to play
> Reached by SaveGameStage's JP ($C2E5) once the shots and the buildings are staged with the game state at $D92B-$FDFB, interrupts off. Keeps SP in SaveReturn's operand, takes GlueStack, sets IX = DiskSave ($4033, the module's save entry; GameLoad passes DiskLoad), HALTs once for a frame's start, pages bank 4 ($14) and sets the ROM's keyboard variables as the module reads them: FLAGS ($5C3B) = $CC, LAST_K ($5C08) = ENTER, CAPS LOCK off (FLAGS2 bit 3). Then JP DiskModule ($C000 in bank 4).
$6905 ; keep SP in SaveReturn's LD SP
$6909 ; GlueStack: bank 4 covers the game's stack
$690C ; the module's save entry
$6910 ; bank 4: the disk module
$6913 ; a frame's start: the music handler has run before the switch
$6919 ; FLAGS: the keyboard state the module's key reading expects
$691E ; LAST_K = ENTER
$6923 ; FLAGS2 bit 3: CAPS LOCK off
$6928 ; the disk module; it comes back with JP SaveReturn
$692B SaveReturn ; back from the disk module (bank 0 paged again)
$692C SaveReturnSP ; the old SP (self-modified operand)
$692E ; IM 2 through ImTable again
$6935 ; a frame's start
$6936 ; PlayLoop's JP EnterPlay: the play screen again
$6939 s Im2Spare ; 48 bytes, never used
$6969 b ImJump ; The JP the vector $6969 lands on: $C3 written by Restart, zero in the block
$696A w ImHandler ; The interrupt handler's address, the operand of ImJump: MusicInterrupt or PlayInterrupt
> Written by Restart and ConstructionMenu (MusicInterrupt), by EnterPlay and StartRobot (PlayInterrupt). Zero in the block.
$696C s ImSpare ; 20 bytes, never used, up to the sprites

@module disk ; the disk load bridge and the stack the bank calls run on
@about Loading and saving go through the disk module, a separate image in bank 4 that reads and writes the saved game on the disk. The game calls it through two bridges: GameLoad here (key 5 of the main menu) and GameSave in the im2 module (key 5 in play). Each keeps SP, switches to GlueStack, tells the module which job to do in IX (DiskLoad $4000 or DiskSave $4033), pages bank 4 and jumps to DiskModule ($C000); the module comes back by a JP to LoadReturn or SaveReturn with bank 0 paged, and the bridge restores SP and IM 2.
@about GlueStack is the stack of every call that pages a bank over $C000-$FFFF (the game's stack starts at $0000 and grows down from $FFFF): SP = $A5ED, growing down over the 54 zero bytes here and over GameEntry's 19 bytes, which run only once, at the block's start.
$A56A c GameLoad ; Load a saved game through the disk module in bank 4
> Reached by LoadGame's JP ($C1FD, key 5 of the main menu). The same steps as GameSave with IX = DiskLoad ($4000): SP into LoadReturn's operand, GlueStack, a HALT for a frame's start, bank 4, the ROM's keyboard variables (FLAGS $CC, LAST_K ENTER, CAPS LOCK off), JP DiskModule.
$A56A ; keep SP in LoadReturn's LD SP
$A56E ; GlueStack
$A571 ; the module's load entry
$A575 ; bank 4: the disk module
$A578 ; a frame's start
$A57E ; FLAGS: the keyboard state the module expects
$A583 ; LAST_K = ENTER
$A588 ; FLAGS2 bit 3: CAPS LOCK off
$A58D ; the disk module; it comes back with JP LoadReturn
$A590 LoadReturn ; back from the disk module (bank 0 paged again), its flags kept
$A591 LoadReturnSP ; the old SP (self-modified operand)
$A593 ; IM 2 through ImTable again
$A59A ; a frame's start
$A59B ; $FFFF into the view buffer's first word, which nothing reads
$A5A1 ; with the module's flags: NZ a game was loaded (NewGameSetup goes straight to EnterPlay), Z a new game
$A5A4 s GlueStack ; The stack of the bank calls: SP = $A5ED, down over these 54 bytes and GameEntry's
> Set by MenuPicture, GameSave and GameLoad while bank 3 or bank 4 lies over $C000-$FFFF. Zero in the block; the pushes reach into GameEntry ($A5DA-$A5EC), which never runs again.

@module start ; the game's entry, the restart, the new game set-up and the play loop
@about The game's top level. The game block is entered once at GameEntry, which runs bank 1's start-up and falls into Restart; every later restart comes back to Restart: the abort key I in play, and the paths no longer run (the tape load's error, the old end of a game, the unused tape code). Restart starts the tune from its beginning, builds the IM 2 table, seeds the random generator and calls MainMenu; NewGameSetup clears the game state and builds the map (a loaded game skips it); EnterPlay draws the play screen and switches to PlayInterrupt; PlayLoop runs one game turn a pass, forever.
@about The routines in calling order: GameEntry ($A5DA) -> Restart ($A5EF) -> MainMenu (the mainmenu module) -> NewGameSetup ($A62D) -> EnterPlay ($A68E) -> PlayLoop ($A69A). Each falls into the next; nothing here returns.
@about The interrupts: Restart writes ImTable ($6800-$6900) full of $69 with I = $68, so every vector is $6969, the JP ImJump, whose operand ImHandler ($696A) switches the handler: MusicInterrupt (the tune) in the menus, PlayInterrupt in play (the frame count, the radar, the sound effects and the tune).
@about PlayLoop is the game's state machine in one loop: WaitKey, MoveShip, WorldTurn, DrawView, GameTurn; then what lies under the ship decides whether a menu opens: our robot with the ship on its top opens RobotMenu (robotmenu module), the pad of our warbase at altitude 15 with a free door opens ConstructionMenu (construction module), and PlayLoop writes the new robot's start state after it. Last the game keys: I abandons the game (Restart), 5 saves it to disk (SaveGameStage, then EnterPlay again).
@about The module owns no data; NewGameSetup zeroes the robot records ($DA00), the variables ($FD04-$FDFC), Buildings and Shots. The bug of the full robot table (the new-robot writes land on enemy slot 24) is at PlayLoop.
$A5DA c GameEntry ; The game block's entry: bank 1's start-up, then Restart
> The block's depacker ends with JP $A5DA. Interrupts off, SP = 0; pages bank 1 ($11) and calls MusicStart ($D8E1, bank 1's own start-up code), pages bank 0 ($10) and falls into Restart. Runs once: GlueStack grows over these bytes later.
$A5DA ; no interrupts until the IM 2 table is built
$A5DB ; SP = 0: the stack grows down from $FFFF, in bank 0
$A5DE ; bank 1
$A5E5 ; bank 1's start-up
$A5E8 ; bank 0 again
$A5EF c Restart ; Every restart: the tune from its start, the IM 2 table, the random seed, the main menu
> Entered from GameEntry and by JP from the abort key in play ($A712); the dead paths jump here too (LoadError $C28A, GameOverWait $AEFD, UnusedTapeCode's return address at $AE30). Interrupts off, SP = 0, bank 1's PlayerInit ($C000) restarts the tune, bank 0 again. Then ImTable: 257 bytes of $69 at $6800-$6900 (B = 0 runs the loop 256 times, the last byte after it), ImJump = $C3 (JP), ImHandler = MusicInterrupt, I = $68, IM 2, EI. RandomSeed = $39,$30,$39,$30. MainMenu returns Z for a new game and NewGameSetup follows; a load does not return here: LoadReturn jumps to NewGameSetup with NZ.
$A5EF ; no interrupts while the table is built
$A5F0 ; SP = 0
$A5F3 ; bank 1
$A5FA ; the player: the tune from its start
$A5FD ; bank 0 again
$A604 ; the IM 2 table: 257 bytes of $69 at $6800-$6900
$A60A FillVectorTable ; 256 bytes, then the 257th below
$A60F ; the JP at $6969: every vector lands on ImHandler
$A614 ; MusicInterrupt: the tune plays while the menu runs
$A61A ; I = $68: the vectors are read from ImTable
$A621 ; the random seed $FD00-$FD03 = $39,$30,$39,$30 (12345 twice)
$A62A ; the main menu: back with Z = СТАРТ ИГРЫ (start game) (a load goes on from LoadReturn)

@module interrupts ; the frame interrupt of play: the radar, the shot sounds, the frame count
@about PlayInterrupt is the handler in play (ImHandler, set by EnterPlay and by StartRobot when the construction menu closes; the menus use MusicInterrupt of the music module). Once a frame: TurnFrames + 1 (the play loop's 10-frame turn), one of the two radar pictures (ShowRadar, by RadarPhase), a frame of the tune (PlayMusic, which PlayInterrupt jumps to in place of its call of ShowRadar and which comes back to it), and one step of the running sound effect (SoundEffect, by SoundFx). PlayInterruptEnd (EI; RET) is its last two bytes.
@about The radar: two pictures of 16 lines x 16 bytes, RadarOwn $D800 (the terrain + the player's robots, shown in $4D, cyan on blue) and RadarEnemy $D900 (the terrain + the enemy's, $4E, yellow on blue), one pixel a map cell for the 128 columns from RadarColumn. Shown in turns every frame at rows 22-23, columns 6-21: the terrain stays, each side's robots blink at 25 Hz in their colour. BuildRadar makes both from the map; ToggleRadarDot (with RadarDotAddress) XORs one robot's 2 x 2 dot in or out.
@about The sound effects are the beeper's: SoundFx is set by the shot code (1 a shot fired, $FC a miss, $FA a hit, $C8 a robot destroyed) and counted on by the interrupt. 2-$7F: a burst of pulses from the ROM's bytes, fewer and further apart each frame, a falling sweep until $80; $C8-$FF: 30 random speaker levels a frame until it wraps to 0. The tune plays on the AY at the same time.
@about The interrupt runs with interrupts off (the CPU's acceptance) and turns them on only at its last EI.
$D566 c PlayInterrupt ; the interrupt handler of play, one a frame
> Installed in ImHandler by EnterPlay ($A694) and by StartRobot when the construction menu ends ($CBA2). Keeps AF, BC, DE, HL (not IX, IY, the alternate set: the routines it calls do not use them). TurnFrames + 1 (TurnPace $AE1D waits for 10, then zeroes it); RadarPhase toggled, ShowRadar with RadarOwn / $4D on phase 0, RadarEnemy / $4E on phase 1; SoundFx not 0: counted on (a tone ends at $80, a noise when it wraps to 0) and SoundEffect plays this frame's step. Ends in PlayInterruptEnd's EI;
$D566 ; keep the interrupted code's registers
$D567 ; (BC)
$D568 ; (DE)
$D569 ; (HL)
$D56D ; one more frame of this turn
$D571 ; its attribute: bright cyan on blue
$D576 ; toggle
$D578 ; kept for the next frame
$D57B ; phase 0: the player's picture
$D580 ; bright yellow on blue
$D588 ; (0: silent)
$D589 ; silent: on to the exit
$D58C ; the tone's end?
$D58E ; no: keep the count
$D590 ; the tone is over: silent
$D595 ; this frame's burst (the flags of the OR above choose its kind)
$D599 ; (DE)
$D59A ; (BC)
$D59B ; (AF); on into PlayInterruptEnd
$D59C c PlayInterruptEnd ; PlayInterrupt's last two bytes: EI, RET
> Reached only by falling out of PlayInterrupt (and from PlayMusic's return through it). Nothing puts its address in ImHandler: outside play the handler is MusicInterrupt.
$D59C ; interrupts on again (the CPU turned them off on acceptance)
$D59D ; back to the interrupted code
@module ship ; the player's ship: its move, altitude, view follow, radar window and map mark
@about The ship is the player's only body: a flying cursor that moves one cell a game turn over the 512 x 16 map, climbs on fire and sinks onto robots and warbase pads, where the robot menu and the construction menu open.
@about MoveShip runs once a game turn from PlayLoop (and from UpdateRobot while a robot is under direct control). In calling order: LiftShipFromMap takes the ship's mark off the map; the X move (MoveShipX), the view follow (FollowView), the Y move (MoveShipY) and the altitude (AutoClimb, ClimbByFire, Sink) follow, each step tested by CheckShipMove; then ScrollRadar moves the radar window, RadarBlink counts and PutShipOnMap puts the mark back, falling into RefreshRadar and BlinkShipDot. MenuRadarTick is the blink step the robot menus call each pass.
@about CheckShipMove gives the highest top under the ship's 2 x 2 footprint: CellTop (the terrain through TerrainTop, then ObjectTop) for the four own cells, ObjectTop alone for the five cells left of and below them, whose anchored objects reach into the footprint. KeepHigher is the shared max. The ship may not stand lower than that top.
@about Data it owns: ShipX ($FD0E, kept in $0E-$1F5), ShipY ($FD0D, rows 1-15), Altitude ($FD10, 0-$30), ClimbTurns ($FD30), ViewX / MapView (the view's left column and its map address), RadarLeft / RadarColumn ($FD1B / $FD1C), RadarBlink ($FD1E), RadarDirty ($FD52), and BuildingTops (the heights of the 9 building types). The ship's map mark is bit 7 of its cell.
@about Tricks: the ship has no record. Its map mark (bit 7) is lifted before the move and set on the new cell after it; the robots' probes read it. The ship's radar dot is XORed on odd RadarBlink ticks on both sides of the move: it blinks every other turn.
$AF11 c MoveShip ; the ship's move from Input: X by right / left, Y by down / up, each refused by CheckShipMove
> Also the auto-climb (ClimbTurns), the altitude (fire +2 up to $30, else -1 down to what stands under the ship), the view follow (ViewX moves when the ship is at view column 13 or 22), the radar window (ScrollRadar) and the ship's mark: map bit 7 cleared on the old cell ($B01D) and set on the new one ($B024), the radar blip XORed. Called by PlayLoop, and by UpdateRobot ($B4A8) while a robot is under direct control.
$AF17 ; Input bits 2-3: down, up
$AF19 ; neither: on to the X move
$AF1E ; no forced climb left:
$AF1F ; nothing to speed up
$AF21 ; one climb turn less (AutoClimb takes another one)
$AF22 ; ClimbTurns - 1
$AF28 ; Input bits 0-1: right, left
$AF2A ; none: no X move
$AF2C ; both:
$AF2E ; they cancel, no X move
$AF30 ; HL = ShipX, the column 0-511
$AF34 ; right: its own bound check
$AF37 ; a column of 256 or more:
$AF38 ; always free to go left
$AF3A ; the low byte:
$AF3B ; column 14, the left limit?
$AF3D ; there: no X move
$AF40 ; test the new column
$AF43 ; a column below 256:
$AF44 ; always free to go right
$AF46 ; the low byte:
$AF47 ; column $1F5 (501), the right limit?
$AF49 ; there: no X move
$AF4F ; carry: a top there is higher than Altitude
$AF52 ; refused: ShipX stays, the view still follows
$AF54 ; ShipX = the new column
$AF5A ; DE = ViewX, the view's left map column
$AF5E ; no borrow for the subtraction
$AF5F ; HL = the ship's column inside the view
$AF61 ; A = it (the view is narrower than 256)
$AF62 ; at view column 13?
$AF66 ; ViewX - 1
$AF6B ; ViewX + 1
$AF73 ; HL = the map row 0 address of the view's left column
$AF74 ; MapView, where DrawView starts
$AF7A ; C = the row, changed below
$AF7B ; the controls of this turn
$AF7E ; bits 2-3 (down, up) ...
$AF7F ; ... to bits 0-1
$AF80 ; neither:
$AF82 ; no Y move
$AF84 ; both:
$AF86 ; they cancel, no Y move
$AF89 ; not down
$AF8B ; down: one row lower on the map (Y + 1)
$AF8D ; not up
$AF8F ; up: one row higher on the map (Y - 1)
$AF91 ; row 0 or 16 (wrapped to 0)?
$AF93 ; off the map rows 1-15: no Y move
$AF95 ; HL = ShipX for the test
$AF98 ; B = the new row (kept by CheckShipMove)
$AF9C ; refused: ShipY stays
$AF9E ; the new row:
$AF9F ; ShipY = it
$AFA5 ; no forced climb:
$AFA6 ; fire decides
$AFA8 ; one climb turn less
$AFA9 ; ClimbTurns - 1
$AFAC ; climb as if fire were held
$AFB1 ; Input bit 4: fire
$AFB3 ; not held: sink
$AFB8 ; at $30 (48), the ceiling?
$AFBA ; yes: no higher
$AFBC ; two units up
$AFBE ; Altitude + 2
$AFC1 ; on to the radar
$AFC6 ; one unit down
$AFC7 ; already at 0: stays
$AFCB ; A = ShipY, HL = ShipX: the ship's own place
$AFCE ; HL = ShipX
$AFD1 ; C = the highest top under the ship (the carry is not used)
$AFD4 ; A = the lower altitude
$AFD6 ; it would go into what is under the ship: stays
