$B06E ; ... (X-1, Y)
$B072 ; one row down ...
$B073 ; ... (X-1, Y+1)
$B075 ; past row 15 ($FD00 is past the map)?
$B077 ; yes: no row below to test
$B07C ; (X, Y+1)
$B07D ; anything anchored there
$B080 ; (X+1, Y+1)
$B081 ; anything anchored there
$B087 ; carry: Altitude < C, the move is refused
$B088 ; HL = X again
$B089 ; C = the top, for Sink
@module controls ; the controls: KeyTable and the joystick into Input, the pause
@about One byte for every input. ReadControls turns the eight redefinable keys of KeyTable and the joystick the main menu chose (Controls $D3E4: 1 keyboard, 2 Kempston, 3 Interface 2) into Input ($FD0C): bit 0 right, 1 left, 2 down, 3 up, 4 fire, 5 save, 6 abort, 7 pause. The joystick bits are ORed over the keys, so the keys always count.
@about WaitKey is the only caller of ReadControls and the one entry every loop uses (PlayLoop, DirectControl, the menus): with the pause key held it waits for another control, and the game stands meanwhile (the interrupt goes on: the radar blinks, the tune and a sound play on).
@about KeyTable: eight records of 3 bytes (the port's high byte, the bit mask, the key code shown by the redefine screen), entry 0 ending in Input bit 7: 1 pause, I abort, 5 save, SYMBOL SHIFT fire, Q up, A down, O left, P right. Then the byte Controls. RedefineKeys ($C34A, the main menu module) rewrites them.
$D37C c WaitKey ; ReadControls; while key 1 (Input bit 7) is held, the pause: wait for another key
> In: nothing. Out: A = Input (also in Input $FD0C); the flags of the last OR A. When the pause key (bit 7) is held it loops in PauseLoop until some other control is pressed with the pause key up, then falls into ReadControls again: the control that ended the pause is this turn's input. Changes A, BC, HL. Called once a turn by PlayLoop, DirectControlLoop, DistanceLoop, ListMenu, WaitRelease, GameOverKey, RobotDirectControl and ConstructionMenuLoop.
$D37C ; this turn's controls
$D37F ; bit 7, the pause, into the sign
$D380 ; not held: Input stands
$D384 ; nothing held?
$D385 ; then wait on
$D387 ; the pause key still held: wait on; else read once more, below
$D38A c ReadControls ; Input from the redefinable keys (KeyTable) or the joystick the menu chose (Controls)
> The eight keys of KeyTable are rotated into C one by one (RL C, starting from C = 1: after the eighth the 1 drops into the carry and ends the loop), entry 0 ending in bit 7. Then the joystick by Controls ($D3E4): 2 Kempston (port $1F, bits 0-4 already right, left, down, up, fire), 3 Interface 2 (port $EFFE, keys 6-0, inverted and reordered), else none. Out: A = Input = joystick OR keys.
$D38A ; the eight key records
$D390 ; to the mask
$D391 ; A = the half row's keys (0 = pressed)
$D394 ; past the mask
$D395 ; past the key code: the next record
$D396 ; not pressed: the AND left carry 0
$D39B ; no carry: the end marker is still in C, the next key
$D3A0 ; 2: Kempston?
$D3A2 ; (yes)
$D3A4 ; 3: Interface 2?
$D3A6 ; (yes)
$D3A9 ; nothing to add
$D3AC ; (port $001F)
$D3AE ; bits 5-7 off: only the five directions and fire
$D3B0 ; on to the keys
$D3B4 ; bits 0-4 = keys 0, 9, 8, 7, 6 (0 = pressed)
$D3B6 ; 1 = pressed
$D3B7 ; the five keys
$D3B9 ; B = them
$D3BA ; A collects the Kempston order
$D3BD ; fire into A
$D3BE ; 9 (up)
$D3C0 ; into A
$D3C1 ; 8 (down)
$D3C3 ; into A: now fire, up, down in bits 2-0
$D3C4 ; shifted ...
$D3C5 ; ... to bits 4-2
$D3C8 ; Input: the byte every routine reads
$D3CB ; A = Input
$D3CC b KeyTable 3 ; the keys, eight of 3 bytes: the port high byte, the bit mask, the key code (shown by the redefine menu)
> The bytes as built: F7 01 31 (key 1, Input bit 7, pause), DF 04 49 (I, bit 6, abort), F7 10 35 (5, bit 5, save), 7F 02 82 (SYMBOL SHIFT, bit 4, fire), FB 01 51 (Q, bit 3, up), FD 01 41 (A, bit 2, down), DF 02 4F (O, bit 1, left), DF 01 50 (P, bit 0, right). A key is LD A,(port byte) / IN A,($FE) / AND mask: Z when pressed. The code byte only serves the redefine screen (it prints the key and refuses one already taken).
> The ninth byte, $D3E4, is Controls: 1 in the image (the keyboard only); MainMenu sets 1-3 by the keys 1, 2, 3.
$D3CC ; Input bit 7 pause: key 1, port $F7FE (1-5) mask $01; the bytes: port high byte, mask, key code (redefine only)
$D3CF ; Input bit 6 abort: key I, port $DFFE (P-Y) mask $04
$D3D2 ; Input bit 5 save: key 5, port $F7FE (1-5) mask $10
$D3D5 ; Input bit 4 fire: SYMBOL SHIFT, port $7FFE (SPACE-B) mask $02, code $82 (a redefine stores $0E)
$D3D8 ; Input bit 3 up: key Q, port $FBFE (Q-T) mask $01
$D3DB ; Input bit 2 down: key A, port $FDFE (A-G) mask $01
$D3DE ; Input bit 1 left: key O, port $DFFE (P-Y) mask $02
$D3E1 ; Input bit 0 right: key P, port $DFFE (P-Y) mask $01
$D3E4 b Controls ; the menu's choice: 1 keyboard, 2 Kempston, 3 Interface 2
$D3E4 ; 1 in the image: keyboard only (2 Kempston, 3 Interface 2)
@module robotmenu ; the robot menu and its submenus: orders, distance, targets, combat, direct control
@about The menus the player gets by landing the ship on top of a robot of ours. They all draw in the right panel (PanelBusy = 1 keeps UpdateStatusPanel off it) and work on IX = the robot record: +$0A the control (1 the player sits on it, 2 direct control), +$0B the order, +$0E the order's target or miles x 2, +$07 the parts, +$0C the strength.
@about In calling order: RobotMenu (from PlayLoop) -> RobotMenuShow (draw) -> ShowOrder, ShowOrderMiles -> RobotMenuChoose. Its items: 1 DirectControlItem -> DirectControl; 2 OrdersMenu -> OrdersMenuChoose -> the distance menu (AdvanceTitle / RetreatTitle, DistanceMenu, DistanceSelect, DistanceShow, DistanceFire) or TargetMenu / CaptureMenu -> DestroyMenuChoose / CaptureMenuChoose -> SetOrder; 3 CombatMenu -> CombatMenuCode; 4 LeaveRobot -> CloseRobotMenu. Every submenu ends in a JP to RobotMenuShow, ОСТАВИТЬ РОБОТА (leave the robot) and a destroyed robot in CloseRobotMenu.
@about The shared parts: ListMenu runs every list (an item every 3 character rows, 2 rows x 8 attributes, $4D normal, $4E highlighted, $4F chosen; PaintMenuItem colours one) and plays one game turn per pass, so the world goes on while a menu waits; ShowStrength prints the robot's strength every pass; WaitRelease waits for all controls up.
@about The texts are PrintInline strings right after the CALL (code 1 Y X a position, 2 A an attribute, 3 the next line in the same column, 0 the end), in Russian (src/res/game/text/game-texts.txt). ShowOrder copies the order's 27-byte name from OrderNames into OrderLine, the string after its own PrintInline call. The order numbers the menus give: 0 stop, 1 advance, 2 retreat, 3-5 destroy robots / factories / warbases, 6-8 capture neutral factories / enemy factories / enemy warbases.
$A720 c RobotMenu ; landing on a robot of ours: ПРЯМОЕ УПРАВЛ. (direct control), ОТДАТЬ ПРИКАЗ (give orders), БОЕВОИ РЕЖИМ (combat mode), ОСТАВИТЬ РОБОТА (leave the robot); the order to +$0B
> IX = IY (the record), +$0A = 1 (the robot stands: UpdateRobot returns at once for +$0A = 1), MenuCursor = 4 (ОСТАВИТЬ РОБОТА (leave the robot) first), PanelBusy = 1. Falls into RobotMenuShow. Called by PlayLoop when the ship rests exactly on the top of a robot of ours.
$A722 ; IX = IY
$A72A ; the robot menu uses only the low byte of MenuCursor
$A72F ; PanelBusy = 1
$ACD7 c ListMenu ; a list menu: HL the attribute of item 0 (an item every 3 rows), E the items + 1, A the first highlighted; down / up move, fire returns A
> Each pass is one game turn (WorldTurn, DrawView, MenuRadarTick, GameTurn, ShowStrength): the game goes on while a menu waits. A destroyed robot (+$01 = 0) drops ListMenu's return address and closes the robot menu (CloseRobotMenu). Up / down move by one item (items 1 to E - 1) with a click $14; fire paints the item $4F, beeps $64 and returns A. Attributes: $4D normal, $4E highlighted, $4F chosen. Needs IX = a robot record.
$ACD8 ; all controls up
$ACDB ; A = the first item
$ACDF ; paint it highlighted
$ACE5 ; +$01 = 0: the slot was freed
$ACE6 ; alive: read the controls
$ACE9 ; the whole robot menu closed, back to PlayLoop
$ACED ; the controls into Input (key 1 pauses)
$ACF0 ; HL = the attribute of item 0 again
$ACF1 ; C = the highlighted item
$ACF2 ; Input: bit 2 down, bit 3 up, bit 4 fire
$ACF7 ; fire: the item is chosen
$ACFA ; down or up?
$ACFC ; neither: one game turn and read again
$AD01 ; A = the new item
$AD05 ; no move: one game turn
$AD07 ; keep the new item
$AD08 ; the old item
$AD0B ; back to normal
$AD0E ; A = the new item
$AD0F ; D = the new item
$AD12 ; in $4E
$AD17 ; beep
$AD1B ; and the attribute address
$AD1C ; the robots and the shots go on
$AD1F ; the view
$AD22 ; the ship's radar blink
$AD25 ; the clock and the turn pacing (at least 10 frames a pass)
$AD2B ; HL = the attribute of item 0
$AD2C ; D = the item, E = the items + 1
$AD2D ; check the robot, the next key
$AD30 ; attribute $4F
$AD32 ; paint the chosen item
$AD37 ; beep
$AD3B ; A = the item chosen
$AD57 c WaitRelease ; wait until no control is held
> Calls WaitKey until Input is 0: every control up. Used by every menu before it reads a new press, so the fire that chose an item does not choose again. Keeps BC and HL; A = 0 on return. Uses WaitKey, so key 1 still pauses.
$AD57 ; kept
$AD58 ; kept
$AD5C ; anything held?
$AD5D ; still held: read again
$AD5F ; HL back
$AD60 ; BC back
$AD61 ; all up
@module construction ; the construction menu, robot placement on the map, the beeper tone
@about The screen the player gets by landing the ship on the pad of our warbase. PlayLoop calls ConstructionMenu; it takes a free slot among our 24 robot records (IY) as the preview, builds the screen and runs its own key loop until ВЫПУСК (start the robot) or ВЫХОД (exit); then the play screen is redrawn and PlayLoop gives the new robot its start state.
@about In calling order: ConstructionMenu (find the slot, the part pictures, the text) -> ConstructionMenuSetup -> ConstructionMenuLoop (fire on a part: PartToggle, RefundPart; a direction: MoveMenuCursor) -> MenuPause after every press -> StartRobot (PlaceRobot) or LeaveConstructionMenu. Helpers: PaintMenuCursor and FillAttributes, PrintMenuResources (PrintMenuResource, PrintMenuTotal), ShowRobotDesign (the part colours and the preview robot drawn by DrawRobot through the view buffer).
@about The data: MenuCursor = (L the column: 0 ВЫХОД (exit), 1 ВЫПУСК (start the robot), 2 the parts; H the part row 0 bipod ... 7 electronics), MenuParts = the chosen parts, one bit a row (the new robot's +$07), MenuResources = the copy of Resources the menu spends, written back only by ВЫПУСК (start the robot). PartCosts holds the eight costs and, 8 bytes on, the resource each part pays from first; the general resource pays the rest.
@about The world stands still while the menu is open: the menu loop runs no game turns, and ImHandler is MusicInterrupt (the tune plays; no frame count, no radar drawing over the menu screen). MenuPause is 10 HALTs after every press; a press inside it is lost. PrintMenuResource patches the row byte of its own inline position string ($CC11) before each print.
@about The tail of the module is shared with the rest of the game: PlaceRobot puts any robot on the map (the robot step, the enemy spawn, ВЫПУСК (start the robot)), ShipCell / MapCell give the map cell of a column and row ($DD00 + $200 x row + column), Beep is the menus' beeper tone.
$C849 c ConstructionMenu ; landing on the warbase pad at altitude 15: choose the parts (MenuCursor, MenuParts), ВЫПУСК (start the robot) or ВЫХОД (exit)
> Called by PlayLoop when the ship rests on the pad of our warbase (Altitude 15) and the door below is free. First looks for a free record among our slots 0-23 ($DA00 + 16 x n, +$01 = 0); with none it returns at once (IY = $DB80, enemy slot 24: the bug of PlayLoop's writes).
> Otherwise IY = the free slot: the preview robot, and on ВЫПУСК (start the robot) the new one. ImHandler = MusicInterrupt (the tune plays), so neither the frame count nor the radar runs; the menu loop runs no game turns, so the world stands still while the menu is open. ClearScreen, then the eight part pictures in column 16 from the bottom (bipod) to the top (electronics), each the second facing of the part's sprite cut to 24 lines and drawn upwards by DrawMaskedUp; the preview's frame attributes ($46, 4 x 9 cells from row 15, column 0); the texts (title, part names, EXIT / START, costs, the resource labels). Falls into ConstructionMenuSetup.
> Returns (after LeaveConstructionMenu) with IY = the slot; PlayLoop then writes the start state. On ВЫХОД (exit) the slot stays free (+$01 = 0, PlaceRobot not called), so those writes do no harm.
