@module world ; the world turn: each robot's step (sight, fire, steering by order, movement) and each shot's flight
@about The war itself: once a game turn WorldTurn lets the enemy commander act, then moves every live robot record and every flying shot. The player never fights directly; everything a robot does on its own is here.
@about Robots, in calling order: WorldTurn -> UpdateRobot (dying blink, RobotDestroyed) -> RobotStep: the step delay, FreeDirections (the probes CanMoveRight / Left / Down / Up over TestCell), then RobotDirectControl, or SpotEnemies (ScanLane, LaneSteps) and fire (FindFreeShot, FireShot), or SteerByOrder (the targets: TargetRobotRecord, FindNearestOpponent, TargetBuildingTable, FindTargetBuilding, ConsiderBuilding; the shared InitNearestSearch / KeepNearest / ColumnDistance; the aim RobotAim, RobotLeanTo, PickOneDirection) -> RobotMove: TurnOrStep (StepPosition, GroundUnderFootprint, MoveShip under direct control), SetStepDelay (StepDelays), PlaceRobot.
@about Shots: WorldTurn -> UpdateShot -> MoveShot (2 cells a turn, the wall test with GroundUnderFootprint, the 3x3 hit test, the damage); FireShot runs the first MoveShot at once. ShotGone / MoveShotGone free the record and set SoundFx.
@about Data it works on, all outside the module: the robot records Robots $DA00 (ours, slots 0-23) and EnemyRobots $DB80 (the computer's, 24-47), 16 bytes each (+$00/+$01 cell, +$02/+$03 X, +$04 Y, +$05 course, +$06 steps left, +$07 parts, +$08 facing, +$09 height, +$0A control, +$0B order, +$0C strength, +$0D ground, +$0E target, +$0F delay); Shots $D7D3, 5 x 9 bytes; the capture points Warbases $FD70 / Factories $FD84 (5 bytes, read from the owner byte +3); the map $DD00 (16 rows of $200 cells: bits 0-4 the terrain type, 6 an object's anchor, 7 the ship). It owns only the tables StepDelays $B61D and LaneSteps $B6B0.
@about Conventions: a direction is one Input bit (1 right, 2 left, 4 down, 8 up) and a set of directions is a nibble in C or E. Side $FD51 (0 ours, 1 the enemy's) is set by RobotStep and decides which tables the searches read. A robot is 2x2 cells but only its anchor (X, Y) carries bit 6, so the probes look for anchors one cell around the cells entered. The map is the only index: a robot or a shot that meets bit 6 asks FindRobot whose anchor it is.
@about Tricks: ScanLane loads HL with LaneSteps - 2 (assembled as ScanLaneDone+1) and steps it by D's bit; the nearest searches keep the best distance and our column in the shadow registers (BC', DE'); a target index $FF read as a building record ($FB added to the table's owner byte, an 8-bit 5 x index) reads $FE6E or $FE82 in bank 0, past the variables, a byte the game does not own; FindTargetBuilding leaves HL past the table, which makes one wrong aim after a new building target (the bug at SteerByOrder).
$B0CA c WorldTurn ; one game turn of the world: the enemy commander, every robot, every shot
> First EnemyCommander (one enemy slot built or re-ordered), then UpdateRobot for each of the 48 robot records $DA00 whose +$01 is not 0, then UpdateShot for each of the 5 shot records of 9 bytes at $D7D3 whose +$01 is not 0. Robots and shots are taken in slot order, the player's slots 0-23 first. Shot record 0 ($D7D3) is the one the player fires from the robot menu's БОЕВОИ РЕЖИМ (combat mode) (DirectControl -> FireShot); the robots use records 1-4 (FindFreeShot).
