@module enemy ; the computer's side: the enemy commander, its robot builds and orders, and the nuclear blast
@about The Insignians have no planner beyond EnemyCommander: once a game turn it looks at one random slot of its 24 robot records and either builds a robot there or reconsiders that robot's order. The nuclear blast that a robot with order 4 or 5 sets off at its target, or the player's АТОМНАЯ БОМБА, lives here too.
@about In calling order: EnemyCommander (first in every WorldTurn) picks the slot; a free slot goes through SpawnPickBase (a random enemy warbase), the door check, the random parts (SpawnCheckWeapons), the payment on a copy (SpawnPayPart, SpawnCheckGeneral), PlaceRobot and the start values, the height (SpawnHeightLoop), CountRobots and UpdateStatusPanel. A live robot goes to EnemyReplan, then EnemyChooseOrder (EnemyCaptureOrder, EnemySetCapture) and EnemyFindTarget (FindTargetBuilding).
@about NuclearBlast (from SteerByOrder and the combat menu) looks for the first intact base in reach (BlastBaseLoop; DestroyWarbase / DestroyFactory of the bases module), then the 9 x 9 round area (BlastRow, BlastCell: robots freed, blocks $11-$14 to debris), the robot's own end, NuclearFlash (the attribute flicker) with NuclearRumble (the noise of ROM bytes), the radar rebuild and the recounts.
@about Data it uses: the enemy's robot records EnemyRobots ($DB80, 24 x 16 bytes), the bases tables Warbases ($FD70) and Factories ($FD84) through their +3 owner bytes, EnemyResources ($FD4A) spent through the copy MenuResources and the construction menu's tables PartCosts (+8: the resource each part pays from) and PartHeights ($D7B4), EnemyRobotCount ($FD49), Side ($FD51) for the target searches. NuclearFlash saves the view's attributes in ViewBuffer ($5B00).
@about Tricks: the enemy builds with one random byte as its whole design and simply gives up the turn when the byte breaks a rule, so its army grows by chance; the placing keeps the door's x / y in the alternate registers HL' / B' across the payment. NuclearRumble plays the bytes of the 48K ROM from $0090 as noise.
$B7F4 c EnemyCommander ; once a game turn the computer looks at one of its 24 robot slots: builds a robot in a free one or re-thinks a robot's order
> Called first by WorldTurn. A random number 0-31 picks the slot; 24-31 (a quarter of the turns) do nothing. Side = 1. The slot's record is $DB80 + 16 * n.
> A live robot goes to EnemyReplan: one with order 0 at once, any other with a 1/32 chance a turn, gets a new order (EnemyChooseOrder: a nuclear robot destroy factories or warbases, the others one of the capture orders; destroy robots when the order finds no target). Orders 4-8 are kept only while the target has changed owner and the robot stands on its door: the capture under way.
> A free slot tries a build at a random enemy warbase (owner $20 in the Warbases table) whose door and the three cells below it are free of bit 6: random parts (exactly one locomotion, at least one and not all four of the weapon / nuclear parts, at least 1, 2 or 3 of those parts while the computer has 0-7, 8-15 or 16-23 robots, EnemyRobotCount $FD49), paid from EnemyResources ($FD4A, through MenuResources and PartCosts like the player's), the shortfall of the other resources taken from the general one, at most half of it (at least 11). A refused design is not retried: the slot waits for another turn.
> The robot is set up facing down, 3 steps, order 0, strength $64, +$0A = $80, +$0E = $FF, +$0F = 1, height +$09 the sum of the part heights $D7B4 (bipod 11, tracks 7, anti-grav 8, cannon 6, missiles 6, phasers 7, nuclear 7, electronics 7); then CountRobots and UpdateStatusPanel. IY is left on the slot's record;
