$B86A ; no weapon: no robot
$B86D ; all four weapons: no robot
$B86F ; B = EnemyRobots / 8 + 1: 1 below 8 robots, 2 from 8, 3 from 16
$B879 ; A = the number of weapons ($B505)
$B87D ; fewer weapons than B: no robot (a bigger army builds only better-armed robots)
$B87E ; spend on a copy: the enemy's resources into MenuResources
$B889 ; D: the shortfall the general resource has to cover
$B890 SpawnPayPart ; for each part in the mask, bipod first
$B894 ; A = the part's index 0-7
$B898 ; E = its cost (PartCosts)
$B8A0 ; the resource it takes first (PartCosts + 8)
$B8AE ; pay from that resource
$B8B2 ; not enough: the rest joins the shortfall, the resource drops to 0
$B8B7 SpawnStoreResource ; the resource's new value
$B8B8 SpawnNextPart ; all 8 part bits
$B8BA ; the general resource may cover a shortfall up to max(general / 2, 11)
$B8C6 SpawnCheckGeneral ; the limit against the shortfall D
$B8C7 ; the shortfall is too big: no robot, nothing spent
$B8CA ; the general resource cannot pay it: no robot
$B8CC ; commit: the copy back to the enemy's resources $FD4A
$B8D4 ; +$0A = $80: an enemy robot
$B8D9 ; place it on the door (x in HL', y in B'): +2 to +4, the cell +0/+1, map bit 6, the radar dot
$B8DD ; the start values; the player's robots get the same at $A6F3, except +6 = 5 there
$B8ED ; order 0: the next visit of this slot chooses one
$B8F5 ; +$0C = 100 (the strength a new robot starts with)
$B8F9 ; no target yet
$B8FD ; the height +9: the sum of the parts' heights
$B904 SpawnHeightLoop ; the next part bit, bipod first
$B90B ; the part heights at $D7B4: bipod 11, tracks 7, anti-grav 8, cannon 6, missiles 6, phasers 7, nuclear 7, electronics 7
$B914 SpawnHeightNext ; all 8 part bits
$B919 ; recount the robots, redraw the status panel
$B920 EnemyReplan ; a live enemy robot: order 0 is replaced at once
$B927 ; any other order is reconsidered once in 32 turns
$B92E ; orders 1-3 (advance, retreat, destroy robots): choose anew
$B932 ; orders 4-8: HL = the target table's +3 bytes, E = the owner bits the order looks for ($B3D5)
$B938 ; HL = the target's +3 byte (+$0E = the target's index)
$B942 ; the target still has the owner the order looks for: choose anew (again the nearest)
$B948 ; the target changed hands (or was destroyed): keep the order only while the robot stands on its door
$B95C ; on the target's door: keep the order
$B95D EnemyChooseOrder ; 3 (destroy robots) unless a target is found below
$B961 ; with nuclear: a demolition order
$B967 ; 5 (destroy warbases) with chance 3/4 (bit 0 or bit 7 set), else 4 (destroy factories)
$B976 EnemyCaptureOrder ; without nuclear: a capture order
$B97B ; bit 0: 6 (capture neutral factories, 1/2), bit 1: 7 (the player's factories, 1/4), else 8 (the player's warbases, 1/4)
$B983 EnemySetCapture ; the choice 0-2
$B989 EnemyFindTarget ; the nearest target of the order from the robot's x ($B34D): D its index, $FF none
$B999 ; found: keep the order
$B99A ; no such target: destroy robots

