$D3C7 InputDone ; plus the keys
@module print ; text: the inline print routine, the font characters, big characters, the numbers
@about All text goes through PrintInline: it prints the bytes after its CALL and returns past them, so every string sits inline in the routine that prints it. The bytes: 0 the end, 1 Y X a position (row, column), 2 A the attribute, 3 the next row in the same column, 4 N the size (high nibble the height in rows, low nibble the width in bytes; 0 normal), anything else a character of the Cyrillic GameFont.
@about The chain: PrintInline (in the alternate registers) -> PrintControl -> SetPosition / SetAttr / PrintNewline / SetSize, or a character: PrintChar (8 lines of the 64-glyph GameFont at $C500, codes $20-$5F, glyph = $C400 + 8 * code) or PrintBigChar (each glyph line repeated PrintHeight times, and each pixel doubled for width 2). SetPrintPos turns PrintRow / PrintColumn into PrintAddr and PrintAttrAddr.
@about The numbers: PrintDigit counts how often a negative power of ten fits into HL; E = $20 means "still in the leading zeros", which print as spaces. PrintNumber5 (the day), PrintNumber3, PrintTensUnits, PrintNumber2 (blank leading zero) and PrintNumber2Zero (the clock, the robot counts) are entries into the same chain; PrintCharKeep prints with BC, DE, HL kept.
@about The control handlers read their operand two stack levels up: PrintControl's return address on top, PrintInline's string pointer under it (POP DE; POP HL; ... PUSH HL; PUSH DE). Variables: PrintAddr $FD04, PrintAttr $FD13, PrintAttrAddr $FD14, PrintHeight $FD16, PrintWidth $FD17, PrintColumn $FD31, PrintRow $FD32.
$D3E5 c PrintNumber2 ; Print A (0-99) as two digits, a leading zero blank
> In: A. HL = A, E = $20, then PrintTensUnits: a space for a leading zero. Used for the resources (PrintResourceValues), the construction menu values and the miles of ADVANCE / RETREAT. A value of 100 or more would print a tens "digit" above 9 (":" and on); the game caps every resource at 99.
$D3E5 ; HL = the value
$D3E6 ; (H = 0)
$D3E8 ; a leading zero prints as a space
$D3EA ; the tens and the units
$D3EC c PrintNumber2Zero ; Print A (0-99) as two digits with a leading zero
$D3EC ; HL = the value
$D3ED ; (H = 0)
$D3EF ; every zero prints as 0
$D3F1 ; the tens and the units
$D3F3 c PrintNumber5 ; Print HL as five digits, leading zeros blank
> Used by $AD90 for the day number (the ДЕНЬ: line). Falls into PrintNumber3.
$D3F6 ; leading zeros blank
$D3F8 ; the ten thousands
$D3FE ; the thousands
$D3F3 ; -10000
$D3FB ; -1000
